I like everyone - close your eyes now  bring to mind a picture of a computer  engineer maybe typing away at a terminal  all right you got it okay now open them  I'm betting that what you thought of  probably did it look like this now it's  true I'm past the age of playing with  dolls but when I saw that Mattel had  created computer engineer Barbie I got  super excited I mean how cool is it that  there is a mainstream toy telling young  girls that they can be successful in the  field of computing I mean it even came  with a super cool book that was talking  about how Barbie is creating a game to  teach kids to play with computers except  when Barbie's sister asked her if she  can play the game Barbies responds oh no  I'm just designing the game  I'll need Stephan and Brian to help me  build it what you're telling me the  computer engineer Barbie can't even code  I was top of that she needs the guys to  save the day a second time when her  computer gets a virus and crashes my  feeling of excitement became horked it's  true women are choosing not to study  computer science but that choice it's  not made in a vacuum it's a choice  constrained by the reality of the world  they live in a world that tells them  from a young age they do not belong some  of those signals can be overt like the  case of Barbies laughable computer  engineering career but some of them can  be more subtle like the case of Alana  King a black female computer science  major every time she went to the  Stanford CS building she would get asked  hey are you lost until finally she got  so fed up with it that she wrote a blog  post no I'm not lost what about the case  of Isis winger when she was featured on  a recruiting poster for her company  software engineering team the ad blew up  with comments but with people saying she  must be a hired model and not an actual  engineer these stereotypes surrounding  us since birth kind of powerful effects  no matter how hard our conscious minds  seek to reject them Stanford  psychologist Claude Steele coined the  term stereotype threat to describe a  situation in which an individual feels  at risk of conforming to a stereotype in  his experiment when African Americans  were asked to identify their race before  taking an aptitude test they perform  more poorly than white students however  if you remove this race question all of  a sudden that difference all that  disappears and these findings have been  replicated many times since in a test of  sports abilities white men perform more  poorly than black men however  you frame it as a test of sports  intelligence all of a sudden that Delta  flips if you remind even women of their  Asian Asst before they take a math test  they'll do better remind them of their  sex and they'll do worse I haven't heard  about any of this research before I  started my first job as a computer  engineer but if you had asked me what  effect I thought stereotypes around  women in engineering could have on me I  would have said none I graduated first  in my class I had several job offers and  I actually liked nerd culture I mean I  build robots for a hobby I tell dorky  Star Wars jokes even though I'm the only  one that ever lasted them and even  though I was in the minority on my team  I felt welcome so the first day I  started until about a year into my job I  I hit a wall it was an assignment I just  could not figure out and with each  failed attempt to crack it I felt a  little more of my confidence drain away  until suddenly I found myself thinking  the unthinkable maybe I wasn't meant to  be an engineer I thought about quitting  every day I didn't realize it at the  time but that right there was the power  of stereotype it turned which should  have been a tiny step back into proof  that every stereotype I'd internalized  over my whole life was correct I wasn't  good enough I didn't belong I knew that  I had to ask for help and I knew it  shouldn't be a big deal I mean my team  was super collaborative but I couldn't  bring myself to do it for me asking for  help  it meant becoming like Barbies it meant  admitting I'd failed and I wouldn't just  be failing myself I'd be failing women  everywhere as one of the few of us I  stood for all of us so I couldn't just  be good I had to be exceptional so that  maybe I could disprove the stereotype  that women didn't belong in engineering  try taking that weight on your shoulders  as a 22 year old I've read research by  Carnegie Mellon at Carnegie Mellon with  students there and the experiences  reported by women were eerily similar to  my own many came in a stellar math and  science students but as they ran into  challenges with their introductory  programming courses they too began to  believe the voices they'd once rejected  voices that told them they'd only been  admitted because they were women it  didn't matter that they eventually  mastered the material they couldn't get  it out of their heads of the women  interviewed half would drop out of the  program but of those dropouts half were  on the honor roll these women aren't the  only one paying the cops here businesses  are missing out two companies that have  more gender diversity have greater  profits and greater sales revenue than  their competitors  studies show that teams that include  women are more innovative and more  productive than those that don't  so if we want to improve our business  outcomes keep women from dropping out of  these programs let's start by addressing  the stereotypes that are turning off not  only women but anyone else who falls  outside of the narrow definition we have  of what it means to be a techie to other  women out there please don't make the  mistake of believing you're the only one  struggling with these feelings ask for  help when you need it get past with  you're stuck on and I guarantee one day  you'll be that person for someone else  to those of you in the majority we need  your help to understand that even under  the near of confidence a person that you  could reach out to is struggling against  an invisible current that they may not  even know is there know that even your  simple nudge of encouragement can make  all the difference that's how I got past  my wall and finally to everyone else all  I ask is this be more mindful of the  stereotypes that you unintentionally  propagate think about it really what it  really means when you tell someone of  course you're going to get into that  program you're a woman or what you're  really saying when you tell someone you  don't look like an engineer the power of  stereotype comes from repetition over  and over again until the idea is  ingrained within our culture which means  we're all guilty of contributing to the  problem to quote from a fellow TED  speaker the problem with our stereotypes  around computer scientists it's not that  they're wrong it's just that they're  incomplete there's space here for  mathletes and athletes cake bakers and  robot makers let's start by widening our  definition of what it means to be a  computer scientist so the next time you  close your eyes you can imagine Barbie  coding alongside Stephan and Brian and  then together we can all build a future  in which we can live long and prosper  [Applause]  [Music]
 

